---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>King Penguin</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <header class="header">
            <h1>King Penguin</h1>
        </header>
        <Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>

                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Aptenodytes patagonicus</li>
                        <li>Size: Approximately 70-100 cm (28-39 inches) tall, weighing 9.3-18 kg (20.5-39.7 lbs). They are the second-largest penguin species.</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Found on subantarctic islands, including South Georgia, the Falkland Islands, and various other islands in the Southern Ocean.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Least Concern</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>The King Penguin's diet consists mainly of small fish, particularly lanternfish, as well as squid and crustaceans. They are deep divers and can forage far from their breeding colonies.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>King Penguins breed in large, noisy colonies on beaches and grassy slopes. They have a unique breeding cycle that lasts over a year. They form monogamous pairs that share incubation duties, holding their single egg on their feet under a brood patch. Chicks take a long time to mature and form large crèches for protection.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>They have striking yellow and orange plumage on their head, neck, and upper chest.</li>
                        <li>King Penguin parents recognize their chick by its unique calls.</li>
                        <li>They undertake long foraging trips, sometimes lasting several days.</li>
                        <li>Unlike many other penguins, they do not build nests.</li>
                        <li>King Penguin populations are relatively stable and have been increasing in some areas.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
    </body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>